I get up when the sky is light. I wash my face and put on my clothes. My bag is next to the door. I check my book and my water bottle. We eat breakfast together. I walk to school with Dad. We stop before we cross the road. My friend waves to me at the gate. I put my bag beside my chair. Now I am ready to learn. A week later, I look at my notes again. Some details make me smile because I had almost forgotten them. I compare what I planned with what I actually did. One part went well, and another part needs more practice. My family asks me to choose just one change for next time. I decide to slow down, check what I need, and ask when something is unclear. My friend makes a different choice. We agree that both plans can be useful. There is no need for everyone to learn in exactly the same way. We promise to tell each other how the next try goes. I draw a small picture beside my notes. It reminds me that learning can happen outside a classroom too. A simple day can teach us how to notice, listen, plan, and care for other people. I close my notebook and put it away. We give each person a turn and make sure that no one feels left out. A small job can teach me to be patient and to pay attention. We check our work together. I notice that a careful plan can make a simple task easier for everyone. I want to remember my water bottle again tomorrow. The next time I try, I will remember to stop and think before I act. We help each other. Tomorrow will bring another chance to practise.',0,N'advanced',0),
(1165,15,N'EN-LONG-02-P1 · 第1段',N'We go to the park on Sunday. The path is wide and clean. I see a tall tree and some small flowers. A bird sits on a branch. My little brother points at the pond. We stand back and watch the fish. Dad gives us some water. We sit on a bench and rest. Before we leave, we put our trash in a bag. The park is a good place to spend time together. Some leaves are round and some are long. I look at them without picking them. A group of children wants to play with a ball. We move to give them room. Mom asks me what I like best. I say that I like the sound of the birds. We agree to come back on another day and see what has changed. At home, I think about what happened. I write a few lines in my notebook. I put the events in order so that I can tell the story clearly. My family listens and asks what I learned. I explain the part that was easy and the part that was hard. We think of a small change that could help next time. I do not have to get everything right at once. Learning takes time, and I can improve by trying again. Before bed, I get ready for the next day. My friend and I can have different ideas and still listen to each other. I notice that a careful plan can make a simple task easier for everyone. I remember this day. I do not need difficult words to tell other people about my day. We feel happy. We give each person a turn and make sure that no one feels left out. I feel glad that I noticed something useful in an ordinary day.',0,N'advanced',0),
(1166,15,N'EN-LONG-02-P2 · 第2段',N'Some leaves are round and some are long. I look at them without picking them. A group of children wants to play with a ball. We move to give them room. Mom asks me what I like best. I say that I like the sound of the birds. We agree to come back on another day and see what has changed. Later, my teacher asks us to talk about a useful habit. I choose the experience from this story. Before speaking, I make a short list of what happened first and what happened next. My friend listens and asks a question. I answer with a real example instead of trying to use difficult words. We take turns so that everyone has time to speak. I learn that a clear story can help another person understand an idea. After our talk, we write down one thing we want to practise. We choose something small enough to do ourselves. If we need help, we can ask an adult. We do not pretend to know an answer when we are unsure. A careful question can be a good way to begin. At the end of class, we look at our notes again. I see that we had different ideas but could still work together. We feel happy. I look around me. A small job can teach me to be patient and to pay attention. We make a plan. I do not need difficult words to tell other people about my day. I smile. I take my time. We stand back and watch the fish. Dad gives us some water. We give each person a turn and make sure that no one feels left out. We rest. We help each other. I put my notebook in my bag and thank my friend for listening.',0,N'advanced',0),
