(1168,15,N'EN-LONG-02 · 全文',N'We go to the park on Sunday. The path is wide and clean. I see a tall tree and some small flowers. A bird sits on a branch. My little brother points at the pond. We stand back and watch the fish. Dad gives us some water. We sit on a bench and rest. Before we leave, we put our trash in a bag. The park is a good place to spend time together. Some leaves are round and some are long. I look at them without picking them. A group of children wants to play with a ball. We move to give them room. Mom asks me what I like best. I say that I like the sound of the birds. We agree to come back on another day and see what has changed. At home, I think about what happened. I write a few lines in my notebook. I put the events in order so that I can tell the story clearly. My family listens and asks what I learned. I explain the part that was easy and the part that was hard. We think of a small change that could help next time. I do not have to get everything right at once. Learning takes time, and I can improve by trying again. Before bed, I get ready for the next day. My friend and I can have different ideas and still listen to each other. I notice that a careful plan can make a simple task easier for everyone. I remember this day. I do not need difficult words to tell other people about my day. We feel happy. We give each person a turn and make sure that no one feels left out. I feel glad that I noticed something useful in an ordinary day.

Some leaves are round and some are long. I look at them without picking them. A group of children wants to play with a ball. We move to give them room. Mom asks me what I like best. I say that I like the sound of the birds. We agree to come back on another day and see what has changed. Later, my teacher asks us to talk about a useful habit. I choose the experience from this story. Before speaking, I make a short list of what happened first and what happened next. My friend listens and asks a question. I answer with a real example instead of trying to use difficult words. We take turns so that everyone has time to speak. I learn that a clear story can help another person understand an idea. After our talk, we write down one thing we want to practise. We choose something small enough to do ourselves. If we need help, we can ask an adult. We do not pretend to know an answer when we are unsure. A careful question can be a good way to begin. At the end of class, we look at our notes again. I see that we had different ideas but could still work together. We feel happy. I look around me. A small job can teach me to be patient and to pay attention. We make a plan. I do not need difficult words to tell other people about my day. I smile. I take my time. We stand back and watch the fish. Dad gives us some water. We give each person a turn and make sure that no one feels left out. We rest. We help each other. I put my notebook in my bag and thank my friend for listening.

We go to the park on Sunday. The path is wide and clean. I see a tall tree and some small flowers. A bird sits on a branch. My little brother points at the pond. We stand back and watch the fish. Dad gives us some water. We sit on a bench and rest. Before we leave, we put our trash in a bag. The park is a good place to spend time together. A week later, I look at my notes again. Some details make me smile because I had almost forgotten them. I compare what I planned with what I actually did. One part went well, and another part needs more practice. My family asks me to choose just one change for next time. I decide to slow down, check what I need, and ask when something is unclear. My friend makes a different choice. We agree that both plans can be useful. There is no need for everyone to learn in exactly the same way. We promise to tell each other how the next try goes. I draw a small picture beside my notes. It reminds me that learning can happen outside a classroom too. A simple day can teach us how to notice, listen, plan, and care for other people. I close my notebook and put it away. I want to try this again tomorrow. Mom asks me what I like best. I look at them without picking them. We move to give them room. We give each person a turn and make sure that no one feels left out. My friend and I can have different ideas and still listen to each other. I keep my notes in a safe place so that I can find them later. Tomorrow will bring another chance to practise.',0,N'advanced',0),
(1169,15,N'EN-LONG-03-P1 · 第1段',N'I visit the library after school. There are many books on the shelves. I look for a story about a small animal. The cover has a bright picture. I read a few lines before I choose it. Then I sit at a table and read quietly. I use a small card to mark my page. I check when I must return the book. At home, I tell my sister about the story. One part of the story is hard to understand. I read the lines before it and look at the picture. My teacher asks me to explain what I think. Her question helps me find an answer. I do not fold the corners of the pages. I keep the book away from food and water. Next time, I want to find a book about trees. At home, I think about what happened. I write a few lines in my notebook. I put the events in order so that I can tell the story clearly. My family listens and asks what I learned. I explain the part that was easy and the part that was hard. We think of a small change that could help next time. I do not have to get everything right at once. Learning takes time, and I can improve by trying again. Before bed, I get ready for the next day. We talk. When I am not sure what to do, I can ask someone who knows. There is no need to rush. We learn something new today. Thanks. The next time I try, I will remember to stop and think before I act. I try again. My friend listens. I tell my family about it. I want to try this again tomorrow. I feel glad that I noticed something useful in an ordinary day.',0,N'advanced',0),
(1170,15,N'EN-LONG-03-P2 · 第2段',N'One part of the story is hard to understand. I read the lines before it and look at the picture. My teacher asks me to explain what I think. Her question helps me find an answer. I do not fold the corners of the pages. I keep the book away from food and water. Next time, I want to find a book about trees. Later, my teacher asks us to talk about a useful habit. I choose the experience from this story. Before speaking, I make a short list of what happened first and what happened next. My friend listens and asks a question. I answer with a real example instead of trying to use difficult words. We take turns so that everyone has time to speak. I learn that a clear story can help another person understand an idea. After our talk, we write down one thing we want to practise. We choose something small enough to do ourselves. If we need help, we can ask an adult. We do not pretend to know an answer when we are unsure. A careful question can be a good way to begin. At the end of class, we look at our notes again. I see that we had different ideas but could still work together. At home, I tell my sister about the story. I keep my notes in a safe place so that I can find them later. I use a small card to mark my page. We learn something new today. Then I sit at a table and read quietly. I look for a story about a small animal. When I am not sure what to do, I can ask someone who knows. We feel happy. I put my notebook in my bag and thank my friend for listening.',0,N'advanced',0),
(1171,15,N'EN-LONG-03-P3 · 第3段',N'I visit the library after school. There are many books on the shelves. I look for a story about a small animal. The cover has a bright picture. I read a few lines before I choose it. Then I sit at a table and read quietly. I use a small card to mark my page. I check when I must return the book. At home, I tell my sister about the story. A week later, I look at my notes again. Some details make me smile because I had almost forgotten them. I compare what I planned with what I actually did. One part went well, and another part needs more practice. My family asks me to choose just one change for next time. I decide to slow down, check what I need, and ask when something is unclear. My friend makes a different choice. We agree that both plans can be useful. There is no need for everyone to learn in exactly the same way. We promise to tell each other how the next try goes. I draw a small picture beside my notes. It reminds me that learning can happen outside a classroom too. A simple day can teach us how to notice, listen, plan, and care for other people. I close my notebook and put it away. When I am not sure what to do, I can ask someone who knows. Thanks. Next time, I want to find a book about trees. I take my time. My friend listens. I notice that a careful plan can make a simple task easier for everyone. We talk. I read the lines before it and look at the picture. My friend and I can have different ideas and still listen to each other. Tomorrow will bring another chance to practise.',0,N'advanced',0),
(1172,15,N'EN-LONG-03 · 全文',N'I visit the library after school. There are many books on the shelves. I look for a story about a small animal. The cover has a bright picture. I read a few lines before I choose it. Then I sit at a table and read quietly. I use a small card to mark my page. I check when I must return the book. At home, I tell my sister about the story. One part of the story is hard to understand. I read the lines before it and look at the picture. My teacher asks me to explain what I think. Her question helps me find an answer. I do not fold the corners of the pages. I keep the book away from food and water. Next time, I want to find a book about trees. At home, I think about what happened. I write a few lines in my notebook. I put the events in order so that I can tell the story clearly. My family listens and asks what I learned. I explain the part that was easy and the part that was hard. We think of a small change that could help next time. I do not have to get everything right at once. Learning takes time, and I can improve by trying again. Before bed, I get ready for the next day. We talk. When I am not sure what to do, I can ask someone who knows. There is no need to rush. We learn something new today. Thanks. The next time I try, I will remember to stop and think before I act. I try again. My friend listens. I tell my family about it. I want to try this again tomorrow. I feel glad that I noticed something useful in an ordinary day.

One part of the story is hard to understand. I read the lines before it and look at the picture. My teacher asks me to explain what I think. Her question helps me find an answer. I do not fold the corners of the pages. I keep the book away from food and water. Next time, I want to find a book about trees. Later, my teacher asks us to talk about a useful habit. I choose the experience from this story. Before speaking, I make a short list of what happened first and what happened next. My friend listens and asks a question. I answer with a real example instead of trying to use difficult words. We take turns so that everyone has time to speak. I learn that a clear story can help another person understand an idea. After our talk, we write down one thing we want to practise. We choose something small enough to do ourselves. If we need help, we can ask an adult. We do not pretend to know an answer when we are unsure. A careful question can be a good way to begin. At the end of class, we look at our notes again. I see that we had different ideas but could still work together. At home, I tell my sister about the story. I keep my notes in a safe place so that I can find them later. I use a small card to mark my page. We learn something new today. Then I sit at a table and read quietly. I look for a story about a small animal. When I am not sure what to do, I can ask someone who knows. We feel happy. I put my notebook in my bag and thank my friend for listening.

I visit the library after school. There are many books on the shelves. I look for a story about a small animal. The cover has a bright picture. I read a few lines before I choose it. Then I sit at a table and read quietly. I use a small card to mark my page. I check when I must return the book. At home, I tell my sister about the story. A week later, I look at my notes again. Some details make me smile because I had almost forgotten them. I compare what I planned with what I actually did. One part went well, and another part needs more practice. My family asks me to choose just one change for next time. I decide to slow down, check what I need, and ask when something is unclear. My friend makes a different choice. We agree that both plans can be useful. There is no need for everyone to learn in exactly the same way. We promise to tell each other how the next try goes. I draw a small picture beside my notes. It reminds me that learning can happen outside a classroom too. A simple day can teach us how to notice, listen, plan, and care for other people. I close my notebook and put it away. When I am not sure what to do, I can ask someone who knows. Thanks. Next time, I want to find a book about trees. I take my time. My friend listens. I notice that a careful plan can make a simple task easier for everyone. We talk. I read the lines before it and look at the picture. My friend and I can have different ideas and still listen to each other. Tomorrow will bring another chance to practise.',0,N'advanced',0),
(1173,15,N'EN-LONG-04-P1 · 第1段',N'We make lunch together at home. Dad cooks the rice and Mom washes the vegetables. I put bowls and spoons on the table. My sister washes her hands before she sits down. We wait until everyone is ready. At lunch, we talk about our day. I try a little of each kind of food. After lunch, I help clean the table. We all have a small job to do. Hot water and sharp tools are for adults to use. I can carry a clean plate and fold a cloth. Mom shows me how to put things where they belong. We take only as much food as we can eat. Dad puts the extra food away for later. I thank my family for the meal. Working together makes the room neat again. At home, I think about what happened. I write a few lines in my notebook. I put the events in order so that I can tell the story clearly. My family listens and asks what I learned. I explain the part that was easy and the part that was hard. We think of a small change that could help next time. I do not have to get everything right at once. Learning takes time, and I can improve by trying again. Before bed, I get ready for the next day. I take my time. I keep my notes in a safe place so that I can find them later. I do not need difficult words to tell other people about my day. The next time I try, I will remember to stop and think before I act. I smile. We feel happy. I ask a simple question. We help each other. I tell my family about it. I feel glad that I noticed something useful in an ordinary day.',0,N'advanced',0),
