(1181,15,N'EN-LONG-06-P1 · 第1段',N'My teacher gives me a seed. I put it in a pot and cover it with soil. Then I add a little water. The pot sits near a window. Every morning, I look for a change. One day, a small green leaf comes out. I am happy to see it. I do not pull the plant. Mom says that it needs time to grow. I draw the leaf in my notebook. I write the date next to my drawing. Sometimes the soil is still wet, so I do not add more water. My teacher helps me learn what the plant needs. My friend has a taller plant, but I do not need to hurry mine. We compare our drawings and talk about the new leaves. Watching a small change can be very interesting. At home, I think about what happened. I write a few lines in my notebook. I put the events in order so that I can tell the story clearly. My family listens and asks what I learned. I explain the part that was easy and the part that was hard. We think of a small change that could help next time. I do not have to get everything right at once. Learning takes time, and I can improve by trying again. Before bed, I get ready for the next day. The next time I try, I will remember to stop and think before I act. We check our work together. We rest. We help each other. I look around me. We give each person a turn and make sure that no one feels left out. I keep my notes in a safe place so that I can find them later. I ask a simple question. I feel glad that I noticed something useful in an ordinary day.',0,N'advanced',0),
