(1184,15,N'EN-LONG-06 · 全文',N'My teacher gives me a seed. I put it in a pot and cover it with soil. Then I add a little water. The pot sits near a window. Every morning, I look for a change. One day, a small green leaf comes out. I am happy to see it. I do not pull the plant. Mom says that it needs time to grow. I draw the leaf in my notebook. I write the date next to my drawing. Sometimes the soil is still wet, so I do not add more water. My teacher helps me learn what the plant needs. My friend has a taller plant, but I do not need to hurry mine. We compare our drawings and talk about the new leaves. Watching a small change can be very interesting. At home, I think about what happened. I write a few lines in my notebook. I put the events in order so that I can tell the story clearly. My family listens and asks what I learned. I explain the part that was easy and the part that was hard. We think of a small change that could help next time. I do not have to get everything right at once. Learning takes time, and I can improve by trying again. Before bed, I get ready for the next day. The next time I try, I will remember to stop and think before I act. We check our work together. We rest. We help each other. I look around me. We give each person a turn and make sure that no one feels left out. I keep my notes in a safe place so that I can find them later. I ask a simple question. I feel glad that I noticed something useful in an ordinary day.

I write the date next to my drawing. Sometimes the soil is still wet, so I do not add more water. My teacher helps me learn what the plant needs. My friend has a taller plant, but I do not need to hurry mine. We compare our drawings and talk about the new leaves. Watching a small change can be very interesting. Later, my teacher asks us to talk about a useful habit. I choose the experience from this story. Before speaking, I make a short list of what happened first and what happened next. My friend listens and asks a question. I answer with a real example instead of trying to use difficult words. We take turns so that everyone has time to speak. I learn that a clear story can help another person understand an idea. After our talk, we write down one thing we want to practise. We choose something small enough to do ourselves. If we need help, we can ask an adult. We do not pretend to know an answer when we are unsure. A careful question can be a good way to begin. At the end of class, we look at our notes again. I see that we had different ideas but could still work together. Every morning, I look for a change. My friend and I can have different ideas and still listen to each other. I look around me. I am happy to see it. Then I add a little water. I try again. I want to try this again tomorrow. We rest. We talk. We feel happy. One day, a small green leaf comes out. A small job can teach me to be patient and to pay attention. I put my notebook in my bag and thank my friend for listening.

My teacher gives me a seed. I put it in a pot and cover it with soil. Then I add a little water. The pot sits near a window. Every morning, I look for a change. One day, a small green leaf comes out. I am happy to see it. I do not pull the plant. Mom says that it needs time to grow. I draw the leaf in my notebook. A week later, I look at my notes again. Some details make me smile because I had almost forgotten them. I compare what I planned with what I actually did. One part went well, and another part needs more practice. My family asks me to choose just one change for next time. I decide to slow down, check what I need, and ask when something is unclear. My friend makes a different choice. We agree that both plans can be useful. There is no need for everyone to learn in exactly the same way. We promise to tell each other how the next try goes. I draw a small picture beside my notes. It reminds me that learning can happen outside a classroom too. A simple day can teach us how to notice, listen, plan, and care for other people. I close my notebook and put it away. I take my time. Thanks. I can use a picture to help me remember the order of the events. We learn something new today. I look around me. Watching a small change can be very interesting. I keep my notes in a safe place so that I can find them later. Sometimes the soil is still wet, so I do not add more water. We compare our drawings and talk about the new leaves. Tomorrow will bring another chance to practise.',0,N'advanced',0),
(1185,15,N'EN-LONG-07-P1 · 第1段',N'I want to make my room tidy. First I put my books on the shelf. Then I sort my toys into boxes. The toys I use often are easy to reach. Mom helps clean the high places. I wipe my desk with a cloth. There is more space on the floor now. I sit down with a book. The room feels bright and comfortable. I know where to find my things. While I work, I find an old storybook. I want to read it, but I finish my job first. I write names on the boxes to show what is inside. My brother borrows a toy and puts it back when he is done. Keeping things in order takes a little time each day. That is easier than waiting until the whole room is messy. At home, I think about what happened. I write a few lines in my notebook. I put the events in order so that I can tell the story clearly. My family listens and asks what I learned. I explain the part that was easy and the part that was hard. We think of a small change that could help next time. I do not have to get everything right at once. Learning takes time, and I can improve by trying again. Before bed, I get ready for the next day. I want to try this again tomorrow. I can use a picture to help me remember the order of the events. There is no need to rush. We give each person a turn and make sure that no one feels left out. We feel happy. We check our work together. I do not need difficult words to tell other people about my day. I feel glad that I noticed something useful in an ordinary day.',0,N'advanced',0),
(1186,15,N'EN-LONG-07-P2 · 第2段',N'While I work, I find an old storybook. I want to read it, but I finish my job first. I write names on the boxes to show what is inside. My brother borrows a toy and puts it back when he is done. Keeping things in order takes a little time each day. That is easier than waiting until the whole room is messy. Later, my teacher asks us to talk about a useful habit. I choose the experience from this story. Before speaking, I make a short list of what happened first and what happened next. My friend listens and asks a question. I answer with a real example instead of trying to use difficult words. We take turns so that everyone has time to speak. I learn that a clear story can help another person understand an idea. After our talk, we write down one thing we want to practise. We choose something small enough to do ourselves. If we need help, we can ask an adult. We do not pretend to know an answer when we are unsure. A careful question can be a good way to begin. At the end of class, we look at our notes again. I see that we had different ideas but could still work together. I do not need difficult words to tell other people about my day. There is no need to rush. My friend and I can have different ideas and still listen to each other. I know where to find my things. Thanks. I take my time. When I am not sure what to do, I can ask someone who knows. My family gives me time to explain what I have learned from this experience. I put my notebook in my bag and thank my friend for listening.',0,N'advanced',0),
