(1193,15,N'EN-LONG-09-P1 · 第1段',N'Dad and I go to the market. We bring our own bag. First we think about how much fruit we need. I see red apples and yellow bananas. Dad checks the prices at two stands. We choose fruit that we can finish at home. I hold the bag while Dad pays. We thank the seller before we leave. At home, we wash the fruit and share it with our family. There are many people, so I stay close to Dad. I ask why some apples are bigger than others. Dad says that the biggest one is not always the best choice for us. We do not buy too much just because the price is low. A little planning helps us use our money well. I want to help make the shopping list next time. At home, I think about what happened. I write a few lines in my notebook. I put the events in order so that I can tell the story clearly. My family listens and asks what I learned. I explain the part that was easy and the part that was hard. We think of a small change that could help next time. I do not have to get everything right at once. Learning takes time, and I can improve by trying again. Before bed, I get ready for the next day. A small job can teach me to be patient and to pay attention. When I am not sure what to do, I can ask someone who knows. I can use a picture to help me remember the order of the events. We feel happy. We make a plan. I look around me. I tell my family about it. There is no need to rush. I feel glad that I noticed something useful in an ordinary day.',0,N'advanced',0),
