Dad and I go to the market. We bring our own bag. First we think about how much fruit we need. I see red apples and yellow bananas. Dad checks the prices at two stands. We choose fruit that we can finish at home. I hold the bag while Dad pays. We thank the seller before we leave. At home, we wash the fruit and share it with our family. A week later, I look at my notes again. Some details make me smile because I had almost forgotten them. I compare what I planned with what I actually did. One part went well, and another part needs more practice. My family asks me to choose just one change for next time. I decide to slow down, check what I need, and ask when something is unclear. My friend makes a different choice. We agree that both plans can be useful. There is no need for everyone to learn in exactly the same way. We promise to tell each other how the next try goes. I draw a small picture beside my notes. It reminds me that learning can happen outside a classroom too. A simple day can teach us how to notice, listen, plan, and care for other people. I close my notebook and put it away. A little planning helps us use our money well. I take my time. I want to try this again tomorrow. We help each other. My family gives me time to explain what I have learned from this experience. Dad says that the biggest one is not always the best choice for us. A small job can teach me to be patient and to pay attention. There are many people, so I stay close to Dad. Tomorrow will bring another chance to practise.',0,N'advanced',0),
(1197,15,N'EN-LONG-10-P1 · 第1段',N'A new child joins our class today. Our teacher tells us his name. I say hello and show him his seat. I tell him where we keep our books. At break time, he looks unsure about what to do. I ask if he wants to play with us. My friends introduce themselves too. At the end of the day, he smiles. He says that he wants to come back tomorrow. At lunch, I ask where he would like to sit. I listen instead of choosing for him. He likes to draw, so I tell him about a friend who likes drawing too. Some of our class rules are new to him. I explain them slowly and give him time to ask questions. Helping someone means listening as well as talking. At home, I think about what happened. I write a few lines in my notebook. I put the events in order so that I can tell the story clearly. My family listens and asks what I learned. I explain the part that was easy and the part that was hard. We think of a small change that could help next time. I do not have to get everything right at once. Learning takes time, and I can improve by trying again. Before bed, I get ready for the next day. My friend and I can have different ideas and still listen to each other. We check our work together. We talk. I ask a simple question. I do not need difficult words to tell other people about my day. There is no need to rush. We learn something new today. My family gives me time to explain what I have learned from this experience. I take my time. I feel glad that I noticed something useful in an ordinary day.',0,N'advanced',0),
(1198,15,N'EN-LONG-10-P2 · 第2段',N'At lunch, I ask where he would like to sit. I listen instead of choosing for him. He likes to draw, so I tell him about a friend who likes drawing too. Some of our class rules are new to him. I explain them slowly and give him time to ask questions. Helping someone means listening as well as talking. Later, my teacher asks us to talk about a useful habit. I choose the experience from this story. Before speaking, I make a short list of what happened first and what happened next. My friend listens and asks a question. I answer with a real example instead of trying to use difficult words. We take turns so that everyone has time to speak. I learn that a clear story can help another person understand an idea. After our talk, we write down one thing we want to practise. We choose something small enough to do ourselves. If we need help, we can ask an adult. We do not pretend to know an answer when we are unsure. A careful question can be a good way to begin. At the end of class, we look at our notes again. I see that we had different ideas but could still work together. I smile. I can use a picture to help me remember the order of the events. We make a plan. I notice that a careful plan can make a simple task easier for everyone. We give each person a turn and make sure that no one feels left out. I keep my notes in a safe place so that I can find them later. A small job can teach me to be patient and to pay attention. I put my notebook in my bag and thank my friend for listening.',0,N'advanced',0),
(1199,15,N'EN-LONG-10-P3 · 第3段',N'A new child joins our class today. Our teacher tells us his name. I say hello and show him his seat. I tell him where we keep our books. At break time, he looks unsure about what to do. I ask if he wants to play with us. My friends introduce themselves too. At the end of the day, he smiles. He says that he wants to come back tomorrow. A week later, I look at my notes again. Some details make me smile because I had almost forgotten them. I compare what I planned with what I actually did. One part went well, and another part needs more practice. My family asks me to choose just one change for next time. I decide to slow down, check what I need, and ask when something is unclear. My friend makes a different choice. We agree that both plans can be useful. There is no need for everyone to learn in exactly the same way. We promise to tell each other how the next try goes. I draw a small picture beside my notes. It reminds me that learning can happen outside a classroom too. A simple day can teach us how to notice, listen, plan, and care for other people. I close my notebook and put it away. We rest. I do not need difficult words to tell other people about my day. At lunch, I ask where he would like to sit. Helping someone means listening as well as talking. My family gives me time to explain what I have learned from this experience. I take my time. Some of our class rules are new to him. The next time I try, I will remember to stop and think before I act. Tomorrow will bring another chance to practise.',0,N'advanced',0),
