At lunch, I ask where he would like to sit. I listen instead of choosing for him. He likes to draw, so I tell him about a friend who likes drawing too. Some of our class rules are new to him. I explain them slowly and give him time to ask questions. Helping someone means listening as well as talking. Later, my teacher asks us to talk about a useful habit. I choose the experience from this story. Before speaking, I make a short list of what happened first and what happened next. My friend listens and asks a question. I answer with a real example instead of trying to use difficult words. We take turns so that everyone has time to speak. I learn that a clear story can help another person understand an idea. After our talk, we write down one thing we want to practise. We choose something small enough to do ourselves. If we need help, we can ask an adult. We do not pretend to know an answer when we are unsure. A careful question can be a good way to begin. At the end of class, we look at our notes again. I see that we had different ideas but could still work together. I smile. I can use a picture to help me remember the order of the events. We make a plan. I notice that a careful plan can make a simple task easier for everyone. We give each person a turn and make sure that no one feels left out. I keep my notes in a safe place so that I can find them later. A small job can teach me to be patient and to pay attention. I put my notebook in my bag and thank my friend for listening.

